@scene(100)

// The simplest possible demo of gradient (Perlin) noise: one sphere, one
// material, one color, both driven by noise.gradient/noise.gradientBump —
// nothing else from the texture library (no cellular noise, no fbm, no
// domain warp, no multiple materials). Good for a quick sanity check that
// gradient noise itself renders correctly before layering anything on top.

ball = sphere(25);

// gradientBump(size) is pre-scaled to stay Lipschitz-safe when added
// directly into a distance field or, as here, used as a material's
// displacement height (see noise.gsl's doc comments for why).
ball.material = material.withDisplacement(material.plastic, noise.gradientBump(12.5) * 0.4);

// gradient(size) is unscaled (~[-1.5, 1.5]); remap it to [0,1] to drive a
// color mix between two colors.
ball.color = color.mixOklab(color.white, color.teal, noise.gradient(12.5) * 0.5 + 0.5);

return ball;
